const TAPSEND_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"TapSend features","description":"Capabilities and which devices support them","href":"features.html","sections":[],"text":"Feature Where it works Pair by QR, pass, link, or manual details Windows desktop and Android. The terminal app and browser also support the pairing methods their interfaces display. Send and receive files Computer \u2194 Android app or phone browser Send to a selected phone, track progress, and confirm saves Windows send queue and the targeted-send CLI Send Later Windows keeps targeted offers for paired phones across desktop restarts Portal Mode Local computer browser for the QR and arrival feed, plus Android or phone browser for uploading Nearby share An Android phone hosts a share that a second phone can open in TapSend or its browser Search and mark files Shift+S in the Windows desktop app Background receiving and Explorer Send to Windows desktop Resume eligible downloads Android app and the phone browser Messages and Scratchpad Windows and supported Android and browser clients Paste Stack Android Share for incoming screenshots and text, Windows to copy them Screen Share, Freeze & Grab, and Live Red Pen Android capture and Windows viewer Throw to This Folder A folder selected on Windows and confirmed when sending from Android Hold This for Me Windows file browser and Paste Stack File Relay Windows sender and destination, with a shared folder on a third computer Linux provides the terminal file browser and core pairing and transfer tools. For Windows-only commands, use the standalone TapSend-Desktop.exe . See the documentation index for individual guides."},{"title":"Quick start","description":"Install, pair, and send your first file","href":"quick-start.html","sections":[{"title":"Install","id":"install"},{"title":"Pair a phone with Windows","id":"pair-a-phone-with-windows"},{"title":"Send your first file","id":"send-your-first-file"}],"text":"Install Windows. Quit TapSend in the tray if it is already open. Run the installer to choose the program location, inbox, private storage, and upload limit. For portable use, extract the complete ZIP, open TapSend-Desktop.exe , and complete First-Run Setup if prompted. Android. Install the matching APK on Android 13 or newer. Android can update an installed copy in place when the packages have matching signatures. Linux. Extract the Linux archive and run bin/tapsend from a terminal. See the terminal guide for its controls. Phone browser. Open the computer's pairing link. Follow browser setup if the browser warns about TapSend's local certificate. See Releases and downloads for the correct package. Pair a phone with Windows Connect both devices to the same local network and open TapSend on Windows. The desktop app starts its server after first-run setup is complete. Press P on Windows to show the pairing QR. On Android, open Tools \u2192 Connect a PC , optionally set a device name, and choose Scan QR . If the phone asks for approval, press V on Windows, highlight its request, and press A . For camera-free setup, copy the pairing pass with Y on the Windows pairing screen. See Pairing and devices . Send your first file Windows to Android Press V , highlight your phone, and press Enter . Press F , open the file's folder, and mark the file with Space . Press S to send the offer. Open TapSend on Android and accept the offer. Watch the desktop queue for the save confirmation. Android to Windows In TapSend on Android, select Computer \u2192 Send files , then pick the files and send the queue. You can also choose TapSend from Android's Share menu. When the phone reports Saved on computer , find the file in your computer inbox. Press R on Windows for the Receive view or check G \u2192 Computer inbox for its location. Press N on Windows for shortcut help. See Sending and receiving files for more transfer options."},{"title":"Windows desktop","description":"Keyboard shortcuts, tray mode, Explorer integration, and camera scanning","href":"windows.html","sections":[{"title":"Install and choose preferences","id":"install-and-choose-preferences"},{"title":"Keyboard shortcuts","id":"keyboard-shortcuts"},{"title":"Check for updates","id":"check-for-updates"},{"title":"Receive in the background","id":"receive-in-the-background"},{"title":"Send from File Explorer","id":"send-from-file-explorer"},{"title":"Scan a phone's share QR","id":"scan-a-phones-share-qr"}],"text":"Install and choose preferences Run the Windows installer and choose where to install the program. The default is C:\\TapSend . Choose a computer inbox, a private data folder, and a per-file upload limit from 1 to 64 GiB. The default limit is 8 GiB. If TapSend finds a saved profile, it offers to keep it. The inbox receives phone uploads, and private storage holds pairing and transfer state. The installer has English and Arabic options. The destination screen shows whether the program folder already exists. You can keep your current storage and pairing settings and choose whether to add a desktop shortcut or command-line access. Run the setup EXE by itself with an internet connection. When you click Install , setup downloads its matching package from GitHub Releases, verifies its checksum, and extracts the files into the program folder you selected. The desktop shortcut, command-line PATH entry, and opening the app after install are optional. If you extract the portable ZIP instead, keep its files together and open TapSend-Desktop.exe . On a fresh profile, use the arrows and Enter to edit the First-Run Setup fields and press S when ready. TapSend starts its server once preferences are saved. After setup, it starts the server when you open the app. tapsend-tui.exe opens the console version and uses its own controls. Keyboard shortcuts Key Action N or ? Open shortcut help D , F , R , T , M Dashboard, Files, Receive, Transfers, and Messages V Choose a phone. In the picker, use arrows or press V again to move, then Enter to select. P , U , Y Open pairing, refresh its QR/pass, or copy the pass from the pairing view Space , S Mark a file in Files, then send the marked files Shift+S Search by filename in the current folder. Mark results with Enter . C Scan a QR displayed by a phone that is hosting a nearby file share L Open or close Screen Share K , Shift+V Open Scratchpad or Paste Stack Shift+R , Shift+H Set a temporary folder drop or hold a focused file Shift+J , : Pair File Relay from the clipboard or open the command input G , W Open TapSend settings or Windows options I Open GitHub release updates Q Quit TapSend Check for updates TapSend checks your repository's public GitHub releases when it starts after setup and every six hours while it is running. A newer stable Windows release with both the installer and ZIP shows [I] UPDATE in the title bar. When tray notifications are enabled, TapSend also shows a Windows notification. In the update panel, press Enter to open the official release and start the update with its installer when you are ready. Press R to check again. Receive in the background Press W and enable Receive in background and show notifications . Close the desktop window. TapSend stays in the notification area and can continue receiving. Click the TapSend tray icon to reopen its window. To stop the server, right-click the icon and choose Quit TapSend . Send from File Explorer Press W and enable File Explorer Send to shortcut . In Explorer, select the files and right-click. On Windows 11, choose Show more options if needed. Choose Send to \u2192 TapSend Desktop . Review the files and select a recipient before sending. Scan a phone's share QR Start a nearby file share on the phone. Press C on Windows and point the camera at the phone's displayed QR. The scanner uses the terminal-style preview and closes after it recognizes the link. Use Esc to cancel. Windows camera permission, the .NET 8 Runtime, and the scanner files included in the bundle are required. If the phone shares one file, TapSend downloads it to the computer inbox. For multiple files, press Y to download all, or press N to choose which ones to save. If the phone still needs to approve your computer, approve it there and press R to refresh. To pair an Android phone with the computer, use the opposite direction. Press P on Windows and scan that QR with the phone."},{"title":"Android app","description":"The transfer home screen, Android Share, downloads, and nearby phones","href":"android.html","sections":[{"title":"Home and Tools","id":"home-and-tools"},{"title":"Share from another app","id":"share-from-another-app"},{"title":"Receive files","id":"receive-files"},{"title":"Share directly between phones","id":"share-directly-between-phones"}],"text":"Home and Tools Computer connects to your paired PC and gives you actions to Send files and View shared files . Nearby phone lets you host or join a direct phone share. Tools opens computer pairing, Messages, and the screen-sharing options. Transfers and their recent results appear in the app as they happen. Share from another app In Files, Photos, or another app, select a file, image, or text and tap Android's Share button. Choose TapSend in the Android share sheet. Review the selection in TapSend. Send files to a computer, or choose Add to Paste Stack for text and screenshots you want available on Windows later. For computer uploads, the phone shows a result once the PC saves the file. If the PC has opened a temporary drop folder for your phone, you can confirm that destination or use the normal inbox. Receive files Open View shared files for regular downloads. When the PC offers files specifically to this phone, review the offer and accept or decline it. TapSend saves downloads under Downloads/TapSend . If the name is already in use, TapSend keeps the original and shows the name it used for the new copy. Share directly between phones Put both phones on the same Wi-Fi network or hotspot. Choose Nearby phone on the sending device and add the files to a new share. On the receiving device, scan the displayed share QR or use its link. A phone browser can open it too. Keep TapSend open on the sending phone until the other device finishes. Approve any extra recipients if prompted, and stop the share when you're done. Nearby shares use local HTTP. Anyone with the live link may be able to request access, so share it only with the intended recipients."},{"title":"Phone browser and Portal Mode","description":"Connect without an APK, use Portal Mode, and understand save confirmations","href":"browser.html","sections":[{"title":"Connect in a browser","id":"connect-in-a-browser"},{"title":"Portal Mode","id":"portal-mode"}],"text":"Connect in a browser Display the computer's pairing QR or link while TapSend is running. Open the link in the phone browser from the same local network. Use Download for shared files, the offer controls for targeted transfers, or Send to computer for uploads. The page also supports Messages and Scratchpad when available. TapSend uses a local HTTPS certificate for computer connections. If the browser asks you to trust it, get tapsend-ca.crt from your own computer's TapSend data folder and install that CA certificate on the phone. Keep the computer's private key on the computer. See Privacy and storage for paths. When a browser manages the final save, TapSend can confirm it handed the file over. Check the browser's download list for the saved file. See Download recovery for interrupted downloads. Portal Mode On the computer running TapSend, open https://localhost:8443/portal.html . If you changed the listen port, use that port. Scan the portal's QR with Android or open its share link in a phone browser. On the phone open Portal Mode, choose the files, and flick the send tile upward. Watch the computer portal. It shows each arrival after the computer's inbox saves it. The arrival feed and portal QR are available from the computer itself. If the portal is turned off, enable Portal Mode in TapSend Settings and restart the session."},{"title":"Pairing and devices","description":"QR codes, pairing passes, approval, renaming, and forgetting phones","href":"pairing.html","sections":[{"title":"Pair with a QR code","id":"pair-with-a-qr-code"},{"title":"Pair without a camera","id":"pair-without-a-camera"},{"title":"Manage paired phones","id":"manage-paired-phones"}],"text":"Pair with a QR code On Windows, press P . The pairing screen shows the code and computer address. On Android, open Tools \u2192 Connect a PC . Enter a device name if you want one, then choose Scan QR . Scan the QR displayed by the computer. If Android requests approval, press V on Windows, highlight that phone, and press A . The phone remembers a successful pairing. Press U on Windows to refresh the QR and pairing pass if they expire or the computer's address changes. Pair without a camera On the Windows pairing screen, press Y to copy the pairing pass. Move it privately to the phone and copy it there. In Connect a PC , choose Paste pairing pass . You can also expand Enter a pass manually and paste a pass or pairing link. The Android form supports the computer address, eight-digit pairing code, and security key when you have those separate details. Manage paired phones Press V to open the device picker. Use the arrows, press V again to move the focus, or click a row. Use Enter to select the highlighted device for sending or Screen Share. Press M inside the picker to manage devices. Use R to rename the selected phone, X to forget it, or C to forget every paired phone. The two forget actions ask for confirmation with Y . A forgotten phone needs a fresh pairing. On Android, Tools \u2192 Connect a PC \u2192 Forget paired computer removes the phone's saved computer connection. If a scan fails, expand Why won\u2019t it connect? and follow the suggested checks in Troubleshooting ."},{"title":"Sending and receiving files","description":"The send queue, Send Later, incoming files, duplicate names, and transfer states","href":"transfers.html","sections":[{"title":"Send to a specific phone","id":"send-to-a-specific-phone"},{"title":"Send Later","id":"send-later"},{"title":"Receive from Android","id":"receive-from-android"}],"text":"Send to a specific phone Press V on Windows and select a paired phone with Enter . Press F and browse to the files. Use Space to mark each file. To search the current folder, press Shift+S , type part of a filename, mark matches with Enter , and close search with Esc . Press S to send the marked files. The target can accept or decline the offer. Check the queue in Files or open the Transfers view with T . State What happened Waiting for phone The offer is queued for the paired phone. It can respond when it opens TapSend again. Accepted / downloading The recipient accepted the offer. TapSend reports progress as data transfers. Saved TapSend received confirmation that the destination saved the file. Browser save unconfirmed The download reached the browser, which took over saving the file. Check the browser's downloads. Declined / failed The recipient declined, the file changed or became unavailable, or the transfer hit an error. Check the row for details. Send Later Use the same V \u2192 F \u2192 Space \u2192 S flow while the phone is offline. Desktop offers survive a restart. Keep the source files in place and unchanged until the phone confirms the save. The PC must be reachable again for the phone to accept and download. For PC-to-PC delivery while the destination is offline, see File Relay . Receive from Android Open TapSend on Windows and leave it running. On the phone choose Computer \u2192 Send files , or share files to TapSend from another app. TapSend normally saves uploads in the computer inbox configured under G . R opens the Receive view. With a folder drop , the Android confirmation shows the chosen destination before sending. Android downloads are saved under Downloads/TapSend . When a name is taken, TapSend keeps the existing file and reports the numbered name it saved. A folder drop on Windows also preserves the existing file. The normal Windows inbox uses a unique incoming filename. For browser-based uploads and the Portal Mode arrival display, see Phone browser ."},{"title":"Download recovery","description":"Resume a computer-to-phone download after closing the app or page","href":"download-recovery.html","sections":[{"title":"Resume a download","id":"resume-a-download"}],"text":"TapSend can reuse parts of an interrupted computer-to-phone download in current Android and browser builds. Resume a download Reconnect to the same computer. Reopen TapSend on Android or return to the same browser profile and TapSend address. Open the same shared file and tap Retry or Download . On supported browser downloads, the progress display can show the amount restored. TapSend matches saved chunks to the file revision. If the computer changes the file, share the new version and start a new download. Android keeps partial downloads in app storage. The browser uses its site storage, which can be cleared or limited by browser policy, private sessions, or low space. A different browser profile or a different TapSend address can use separate storage. Recovery covers supported downloads from the computer. If an upload from the phone fails, send that upload again."},{"title":"Messages and Scratchpad","description":"Send a message or copy a reusable snippet","href":"messages.html","sections":[],"text":"Open M on Windows or Tools \u2192 Messages on Android to exchange short messages. The phone browser also has a Messages section. Use Scratchpad for reusable text such as URLs, addresses, and commands. On Windows, choose the phone with V and open Scratchpad with K . Copy some text in Windows and press P inside Scratchpad to share it with the selected phone. Use \u2191/\u2193 and Enter to copy an item. Press R in Scratchpad to refresh. On Android, open Messages and expand Scratchpad . The phone browser has its own Scratchpad section below Messages. For screenshots or text shared to TapSend through Android's Share menu, use Paste Stack ."},{"title":"Paste Stack","description":"Queue screenshots and text from Android, even while the PC is offline","href":"paste-stack.html","sections":[{"title":"Add items from Android","id":"add-items-from-android"},{"title":"Copy items on Windows","id":"copy-items-on-windows"}],"text":"Add items from Android Share a screenshot or text from another Android app. Choose TapSend \u2192 Add to Paste Stack . TapSend queues the item on the phone if the paired PC is offline and retries in the background. Reopen TapSend if Android has delayed background delivery. Copy items on Windows Press Shift+V , select an item with the arrows, and press Enter to copy it. Paste in the app you were using with Ctrl+V . Press U or D to move items up or down, or X to move one to the end. The queue and its order persist across desktop restarts. Screen Share grabs also appear here. For items you only want to see when you reopen a folder or reconnect a phone, use Hold This for Me ."},{"title":"Screen Share","description":"Start, stop, and troubleshoot the live Android viewer","href":"screen-share.html","sections":[{"title":"Start a live session","id":"start-a-live-session"}],"text":"Screen Share shows a paired Android phone's screen in the Windows viewer. The Windows bundle includes the viewer files and requires the .NET 8 Runtime. Start a live session On Windows, click Screen Share in the title bar or press L . Choose the phone if the picker opens. On Android, open Tools \u2192 Share screen to PC . Review and accept Android's capture prompt. Choose the app or screen scope offered by Android. Press Esc in the viewer, press L again in TapSend, or tap Stop screen sharing on Android to end the session. The live stream is displayed without automatic recording. Freeze & Grab lets you save a frame deliberately, and Live Red Pen adds temporary annotations with the phone's permission. If the stream stutters, check that both sides use matching current builds and that the phone can reach the computer over a stable local connection. Changing content produces new frames. The rate depends on the phone and connection."},{"title":"Freeze & Grab","description":"Crop a screen frame or extract text on Windows","href":"freeze-and-grab.html","sections":[],"text":"Open a live Screen Share session. Press Space in the Windows viewer to freeze the displayed frame. Drag with the left mouse button to select an area. With no drag selection, the grab uses the full captured frame. Press G to save an image or T to read the text with Windows text recognition. In the main TapSend window, press Shift+V to open Paste Stack. Select the result and press Enter to copy it. Press Space again in the viewer to return to the live stream. For text recognition, install Windows OCR support for the language on the screen and select a larger, clearer area if words are missed."},{"title":"Live Red Pen","description":"Send a temporary arrow to a phone that allows annotations","href":"live-red-pen.html","sections":[],"text":"Send a temporary arrow to a phone while it shares its screen. The phone chooses whether to allow arrows for that session. On Windows, open Screen Share for the paired phone. On Android, choose Tools \u2192 Share screen with Live Red Pen . If Settings opens, allow TapSend to Display over other apps , return to TapSend, and select the same Tools action again. Accept Android's full-screen capture prompt. Right-drag with the mouse in the live Windows viewer. Release to send the arrow to the phone. It disappears automatically. Choose Share screen to PC on Android for an ordinary session with annotations off. Stop the screen share to end both the stream and annotations."},{"title":"Throw to This Folder","description":"Route Android uploads to a folder you choose","href":"folder-drop.html","sections":[],"text":"Use a folder on Windows as a temporary destination for one paired phone. Press V and select the phone. Press F , navigate into the destination folder, and press Shift+R . On Android, send a file from TapSend or use Android's Share \u2192 TapSend flow. Review the displayed Windows folder. Choose Send here to use it, Use inbox for the default location, or Cancel . The drop stays available for ten minutes. If it changes or expires before Android starts an upload, choose the destination again. Files that arrive with an existing name receive a numbered suffix such as report (2).pdf , and the Android result shows the saved name."},{"title":"Hold This for Me","description":"Bring back a file or Paste Stack item when you need it","href":"hold-this-for-me.html","sections":[{"title":"Hold a screenshot or text","id":"hold-a-screenshot-or-text"},{"title":"Hold a file","id":"hold-a-file"}],"text":"Hold a Paste Stack item or a reference to a file until you return to the right place. Hold a screenshot or text In Files , open the folder you want to return to, or choose a paired phone with V . Press Shift+V to open Paste Stack and select the item. Press H to hold the item until you revisit that folder, or J to hold it until the selected phone reconnects. Hold a file In Files , focus the file and press Shift+H . When you return to this folder, its reference becomes available in Paste Stack. Keep the original file in place if you want to copy it later. Held items remain across restarts. Press B inside Paste Stack to bring back every held item immediately."},{"title":"File Relay","description":"Deliver a file between Windows PCs using an always-on shared folder","href":"file-relay.html","sections":[{"title":"Set up the route","id":"set-up-the-route"},{"title":"Send and confirm a file","id":"send-and-confirm-a-file"}],"text":"File Relay delivers a file between two Windows computers when the destination is asleep or TapSend is closed there. A third computer needs to stay on and provide a shared folder both endpoints can write to. That folder holds encrypted file contents until the destination returns. Set up the route Confirm the sending PC and destination PC can open the same writable network share in File Explorer, for example \\\\relay-pc\\share . On the destination PC , open TapSend. Press : and run relay create \\\\relay-pc\\share with the real share path. TapSend copies a relay pass to the clipboard. Move the pass privately to the sending PC . Copy it there and press Shift+J in TapSend. The pass gives access to the encrypted lane. Share it only with the sending endpoint you trust. Send and confirm a file On the sending PC, press F and focus a file. Press : and run relay send . Run relay status to see the latest delivery. Holding means the file is staged on the relay for the destination. Start TapSend on the destination PC. It collects the file into the Relay folder inside its configured inbox. The sender's status changes to SAVED once the destination confirms. Keep the shared folder available until the destination collects the file. File Relay currently uses Windows endpoints and a network share. For delivery from Windows to an offline paired Android phone, use Send Later ."},{"title":"Releases and downloads","description":"GitHub releases and the current workspace builds","href":"releases.html","sections":[{"title":"Current workspace builds","id":"current-workspace-builds"},{"title":"Published on GitHub","id":"published-on-github"},{"title":"Update an existing installation","id":"update-an-existing-installation"}],"text":"Current workspace builds The following packages are in this checkout's dist/ folder. Install the matching Windows and Android builds for the newest workflows. Platform File Windows x64 installer TapSend-v1.0.2-windows-x64-setup.exe Windows x64 portable ZIP and web installer package TapSend-v1.0.2-windows-x64.zip Android 13+ TapSend-Android-Upload-Limits.apk The Windows setup EXE needs an internet connection and the matching ZIP published under GitHub release v1.0.2 . End users run the EXE by itself. For a portable installation, extract the ZIP and run TapSend Desktop. The dist/ files are local build outputs. See the published release links below or build from source . Published on GitHub Release Summary v1.0.2 Windows x64 Standalone desktop TUI, web installer, send queue, device management, and background receiving. Download the setup EXE or portable ZIP . Read the v1.0.2 release notes . v1.0.1 Windows IP pairing fix beta Windows ZIP with updated pairing IP and certificate handling. v1.0.1 release notes and assets v1.0.0 The initial public packages for Linux x86_64, Windows x64, and Android 13+. Includes core local transfers, messages, pairing, and Portal Mode. v1.0.0 release notes and assets Compare a downloaded asset with the SHA-256 shown on its release page or in its checksum file, when available. On Windows, run Get-FileHash <file> -Algorithm SHA256 in PowerShell. Update an existing installation Windows. Quit TapSend from the tray and run the updated setup EXE. Choose your install folder and keep your existing profile when prompted. For portable installs, extract the full updated ZIP and start TapSend-Desktop.exe from that folder. If you use Explorer Send to , check that its shortcut opens the updated location. Android. Android accepts an in-place update when the signing key matches the installed app. The workspace APK uses a debug signature. To switch between builds signed by different keys, uninstall the old app first. Uninstalling removes its app-private pairing and queued items. Pair again after installing the new build. For release work, see Release maintenance ."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","description":"Connection, install, save, and playback problems","href":"troubleshooting.html","sections":[{"title":"The phone cannot pair or reconnect","id":"the-phone-cannot-pair-or-reconnect"},{"title":"The app still looks like the older build","id":"the-app-still-looks-like-the-older-build"},{"title":"A transfer seems stuck or the file is missing","id":"a-transfer-seems-stuck-or-the-file-is-missing"},{"title":"Screen Share is missing, blank, or slow","id":"screen-share-is-missing-blank-or-slow"},{"title":"File Relay still says Holding","id":"file-relay-still-says-holding"}],"text":"The phone cannot pair or reconnect Confirm TapSend is running and both devices are on a local network that allows them to reach each other. A guest Wi-Fi network can isolate devices. On Windows, check that the firewall allows TapSend on the active network profile and that the listen port, normally 8443 , is reachable. If the computer's IP changed, restart TapSend and display a fresh pairing QR with P and U . Scan it again. If the wrong network adapter appears in the pairing link, set Pairing host to the correct local address in Settings. If Android asks for approval, press V on Windows, highlight the request, and press A . If camera scanning is unavailable, copy the pairing pass from the Windows pairing screen with Y . On Android, Connect a PC \u2192 Why won\u2019t it connect? shows checks for the current connection. The app still looks like the older build On Windows, quit the old instance from the notification area. Extract the new ZIP together and open the new folder's TapSend-Desktop.exe . If launching through Explorer, check the Send to shortcut too. On Android, check which APK you installed. See Releases and downloads if Android rejects an update. A transfer seems stuck or the file is missing Waiting for phone. Open TapSend on the selected phone and respond to the offer. Keep the computer reachable and the original file in place. Browser save unconfirmed. Check the browser's downloads. The browser controls the final save when it takes over. Saved under a different name. Android and Windows folder drops keep the existing file and report the numbered copy. Check Downloads/TapSend on Android or the inbox or drop folder shown for a Windows upload. Interrupted download. Open the same file from the same connection and retry. See Download recovery for storage and revision requirements. Folder drop expired. Set the folder again with Shift+R and confirm the refreshed destination on Android. Screen Share is missing, blank, or slow Use the current builds on both devices. Click Screen Share or press L on Windows, choose the intended phone, then begin capture from Tools on Android. If the viewer does not open, make sure the full Windows ZIP was extracted and the .NET 8 Runtime is present. If the viewer opens but waits, confirm the Android capture prompt. Start a new session if Android has revoked that permission. Some protected apps can appear blank. If motion stutters, check local Wi-Fi quality and close competing large transfers. Frame rate varies with the devices and network. If arrows never appear, choose Share screen with Live Red Pen on Android, grant Display over other apps , and start the share again. File Relay still says Holding Verify both computers can write to the same network share and that the relay computer is awake. Start TapSend on the destination and check the Relay folder inside its inbox. Run relay status on the sender again. A missing or invalid confirmation needs investigation before the sender can treat the transfer as saved."},{"title":"Privacy and storage","description":"Local data, pairing protection, and where files go","href":"privacy-and-storage.html","sections":[{"title":"Connections","id":"connections"},{"title":"Where information is stored","id":"where-information-is-stored"}],"text":"Connections Computer \u2194 Android or phone browser. TapSend runs a local HTTPS server. Android checks the certificate fingerprint from pairing. A browser may require trust in the computer's local TapSend CA. Pairing links and passes are credentials. Share them privately and refresh them when needed. Nearby phone sharing. The share uses local HTTP. Use a trusted Wi-Fi network or hotspot and stop the share after use. Screen Share. Android prompts for capture and the chosen Windows viewer receives the frames. A regular session streams without automatic recording. A grab is stored on the PC only when you ask to save an image or recognized text. Live Red Pen also requires a separate Android opt-in. File Relay. The relay folder carries encrypted contents. The destination confirms the save with an authenticated receipt. Keep the relay pass private because it contains the route and the key needed to read that route's transfers. Where information is stored Location What's there Windows %APPDATA%\\TapSend\\data\\ by default Computer certificate and private keys, paired devices, transfer records, outgoing offers, Scratchpad, and Paste Stack Linux ~/.local/share/tapsend/ by default Computer data for the terminal app and server Computer inbox Normal Android and browser uploads. Check Settings \u2192 Computer inbox for the actual path. File Relay saves under its Relay subfolder. Android Downloads/TapSend Finished downloads saved by the Android app Android app-private storage Pairing data, partial downloads, and pending Paste Stack deliveries Phone browser site storage Browser pairing and eligible partial download chunks For browser setup, move tapsend-ca.crt from your own computer to your phone. Keep server.key and the CA signing key private. If you change the private data directory or replace the certificate identity, use Pairing and devices to reconnect."},{"title":"CLI and settings","description":"Commands and persistent settings","href":"cli-and-settings.html","sections":[{"title":"Commands","id":"commands"},{"title":"Send flow","id":"send-flow"},{"title":"Terminal settings","id":"terminal-settings"},{"title":"File Relay commands","id":"file-relay-commands"}],"text":"This document describes the command-line interface and persistent settings. For Windows desktop shortcuts, see Windows desktop . Commands --device ( -d ) accepts a saved device name or ID. Quote names containing spaces. If a name is ambiguous, use the ID shown by tabsend devices . --path ( -p ) accepts a local file path. Repeat it for several files. You can mix named options with one positional value, as in tabsend send -d \"My Phone\" photo.jpg . With two positional values, use DEVICE PATH order. Quote paths containing spaces. Use -- before a positional path beginning with - . When a value is missing in an interactive terminal, TapSend prompts for it. In scripts, provide the recipient and file paths explicitly. Run tabsend send --help for examples. Send flow Resolve the device name to a unique paired device ID and validate each path before opening a share. Start a share restricted to that device. A previously paired Android app reconnects without a new QR code. Review the offer on the phone and accept or decline it. Use tabsend status to check progress. TapSend reports a confirmed save when the phone finishes writing the file. Keep the sender command running until completion or cancellation. The standalone Windows app has a separate persistent Send Later queue. Terminal settings Press G for Settings. Use arrows or Tab to move, Enter to edit or toggle, D to load defaults, S to save, and Esc to close. Press Esc again to discard unsaved changes. The CLI equivalents are tabsend settings , tabsend settings set , tabsend settings defaults , and tabsend settings reset . Section Setting Default and behavior Transfers Computer inbox folder Current TapSend data directory's inbox/ ; controls files uploaded to the computer Browser Starting folder Current directory by default; must be an absolute path when set Browser Show hidden files Off by default; applies when the TUI opens and immediately after saving Connection Listen address :8443 by default; binds the server to an address and port Connection Pairing host Automatic by default; overrides the host/IP in pairing links and QR codes, without changing the bind address Devices Direct joins 2 by default; number of paired phones that can join before sender approval, from 1 to 8 Storage Private data folder Platform-specific TapSend data directory. See Privacy and storage . Transfers Upload limit 8 GiB by default on the server. The setting accepts 1\u201364 GiB. Client limits can also apply. Connection Portal Mode Enabled by default. Change it with --portal-enabled=false or through Settings. Connection and direct join changes take effect when a new share starts. The pairing host must be reachable from the phone and covered by the existing server certificate; TapSend reports a mismatch when a share starts. The starting folder and hidden-file preference apply to the terminal interface; the Android download folder remains controlled by the Android app. Android downloads go to Downloads/TapSend . The computer inbox setting applies to uploads received by the computer. File Relay commands The CLI exposes relay create SHARED_FOLDER , relay connect (reads the pass from standard input), relay send FILE , and relay status . See the File Relay guide for the Windows walkthrough."},{"title":"TapSend console TUI","description":"Controls for the console app on Linux and Windows","href":"tui-2.html","sections":[],"text":"This page describes the console interface on Linux and Windows. On Windows, run tapsend-tui.exe inside Windows Terminal or Command Prompt. Use the Windows desktop guide for the standalone TapSend-Desktop.exe window and its letter shortcuts. On Windows, C scans a QR displayed by a phone that is hosting a nearby share. Press Esc to cancel or R to refresh the file list after the scan. TapSend downloads a single shared file directly. For multiple files, press Y to download all, or press N and use arrows and Space to choose files, then press Enter to download. Keep the scanner files alongside the TapSend executables. The scanner requires the .NET 8 Runtime and Windows camera permission. For computer pairing, scan the computer's QR from Android. On Linux, build with cargo build --release --manifest-path tui/Cargo.toml and go build -o tui/target/release/tapsend-server ./cmd/tapsend-server and go build -o tui/target/release/tapsend ./cmd/tapsend , then run ./tui/target/release/tapsend in an interactive terminal. The terminal interface targets Linux terminals and Windows 10/11 VT-capable consoles. Windows Terminal is recommended. No Nerd Font or GUI dependency is required. Use at least 48 columns by 12 rows; the persistent sidebar appears at 100 columns. The reference dashboard appears at 130 columns by 34 rows; 160 by 45 or larger is recommended. It includes Unicode icons, solid borders, five metric panels, transfer rows, a vertical network graph, device/file panels and connection logs. Key Action 1\u20135 / Tab Dashboard, Files, Devices, Transfers, Messages F / D Files / devices; D also requests a device refresh Up/Down, J/K Browse files Up/Down on Dashboard Select a transfer in the queue Space on Dashboard Open the selected completed received file (RX) with the OS default app Enter / Backspace Open folder / parent folder Space Mark a file S Start receiving; on Files, start sharing selected/focused files A Add selected/focused files from Files while sharing P Cycle pairing QR, manual details, and current page C (Windows) Scan a phone-share QR with the laptop camera R New pairing code T Stop/resume downloads (not resumable per-file pause) M Compose a message G / ? / ; Settings / help / command palette Q / Ctrl+C Exit and restore terminal settings Settings controls the inbox, private data folder, starting folder, hidden files, listen address, pairing host, direct join limit, upload size limit, and local Portal Mode. Press G, use arrows and Enter to edit, and press S to save. Changing the private data folder starts a new device identity after restarting the share. Connection and upload settings apply to new shares, not an active session. Transfer statistics come from the server. TX means computer to phone; RX means phone to computer. Progress and ETA describe individual HTTP requests, including parallel ranges, rather than claiming a whole file is complete. served means the HTTP response finished; saved means an incoming file reached the inbox. Unknown lengths display no percentage or ETA. Network history uses the recent bounded log window, auto-scales, and shows last-reported rates. RTT is the latest phone report; absent measurements remain unavailable. Saved devices are not automatically labelled online. Windows builds and terminal navigation were checked locally. Linux and Windows CI checks are defined in .github/workflows/tui-check.yml ; they run after these changes are pushed. Linux runtime and separate Windows 10/11 device tests are still required; CI runners do not replace those checks."},{"title":"Development","description":"Code layout, build commands, and checks","href":"development.html","sections":[{"title":"Source layout","id":"source-layout"},{"title":"Build","id":"build"},{"title":"Verify changes","id":"verify-changes"}],"text":"Source layout Path Responsibility tui/ Rust terminal UI, Windows desktop host, file picker, and desktop controls cmd/tapsend/ Go command-line launcher and commands cmd/tapsend-server/ Local server entry point internal/server/ HTTPS transfers, pairing routes, desktop control, Paste Stack, and Screen Share internal/server/web/ Phone browser and Portal Mode internal/relay/ Encrypted File Relay queue and save confirmations android/ Android sharing, pairing, recovery, capture, and annotations camera-scanner/ Windows QR scanner and live screen viewer Build Requirements for this checkout include Go 1.24 or newer, Rust with edition 2024 support, and platform build tools. The Windows scanner and screen viewer use the .NET 8 SDK to build and the .NET 8 Runtime to run. Android uses Java 17, SDK Platform 35, and Android Build Tools 35.0.0. On Linux, run make build and launch ./bin/tapsend . Run make portable-linux after installing the x86_64-unknown-linux-musl Rust target to create the portable archive. On Windows, run these commands from the repository root. Copy tui/target/release/tapsend-desktop.exe into dist/windows as TapSend-Desktop.exe . Copy the Rust tapsend.exe into the same folder as tapsend-tui.exe . Put the published camera helper files directly alongside the desktop EXE and preserve their runtime folder structure. For Android, open android/ in Android Studio and build the app module, or run ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug from that directory on a shell that can run the checked-in wrapper. The APK is under android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/ . Verify changes Smoke-test the relevant workflow using the packaged binaries. An emulator can test Android UI and capture. Check Wi-Fi behavior, camera permissions, save handling, and installation on the devices you plan to support before publishing. See Release maintenance for packaging and signing."},{"title":"Release maintenance","description":"Build, verify, sign, and publish packages","href":"releasing.html","sections":[{"title":"Prepare a version","id":"prepare-a-version"},{"title":"Build the packages","id":"build-the-packages"},{"title":"Verify and publish","id":"verify-and-publish"}],"text":"Use Releases and downloads for the published release history and workspace builds. Prepare a version Update Android's versionCode and versionName , the Rust package version, the Go CLI version, the Windows installer version, archive names, and release notes together. Run the checks in Development . Build the packages Linux. Run make portable-linux after installing the x86_64-unknown-linux-musl Rust target. Check that the archive includes the CLI, terminal app, server, and installer script. Windows. Run the Windows package workflow with the version you plan to tag. It packages the Go, Rust, and .NET files and pins the ZIP's checksum inside the installer. For a local build, follow Development to stage the Windows files, then run pwsh -File packaging/build-windows-installer.ps1 -ReleaseVersion 1.0.2 . Upload the matching ZIP, installer, and checksum file to the same GitHub release tag, with their generated filenames intact. Android. Assemble the release APK using the existing signing key and check it with apksigner verify --verbose . Keep the keystore and passwords private and backed up. Updates to an installed release need the same key. Windows update notifications look for a stable public release tagged vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH that has both the installer and matching ZIP. The packaging workflow stamps the GitHub repository and version into the Windows CLI used by the desktop updater. For a different version in a local build, set both values on the Go CLI using -ldflags \"-X main.releaseVersion=VERSION -X main.releaseRepository=OWNER/REPO\" before packaging. Add your own packaging/tapsend.ico to use it in the setup EXE, installed shortcuts, desktop window, and notification area. Verify and publish Install or extract the packages on their target devices. Verify startup, pairing, sends in both directions, save confirmations, and any features listed in the release notes. On Windows, open the actual packaged EXE and verify that Screen Share loads with its companion files. Generate SHA256SUMS.txt for the final assets. Publish the verified packages and checksums with the intended tag. List which platforms are available and identify which workflows the packages contain."}];
